<main id="main">
<section class="phero"><div class="phero-media"><img src="/assets/img/photos/storm-collapsed-house.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/storm-collapsed-house-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/storm-collapsed-house.webp 1021w" sizes="100vw" width="1021" height="681" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"></div><div class="wrap phero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><i aria-hidden="true">›</i><a href="/nh-appraisal-services/">Services</a><i aria-hidden="true">›</i><span aria-current="page">Microburst &amp; Windstorm Losses</span></nav><p class="kicker">Appraisal service</p><h1>Wind Damage Insurance Appraisal in New Hampshire</h1><p class="lede">New Hampshire’s worst wind rarely comes with a name. Autumn gales, summer microbursts and the odd tornado do the damage, and the estimates that follow often stop at whatever blew off first.</p><div class="btns"><a class="btn btn-gold" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg class="ico" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> Call (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn btn-line-light" href="/contact-nh-appraiser/">Free claim review</a></div><ul class="phero-chips"><li><small>Common causes</small><b>Microbursts, gales</b></li><li><small>Rare but real</small><b>Tornadoes</b></li><li><small>Watch for</small><b>Wind deductibles</b></li></ul></div></section>
<div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose">
<h2>Straight-line, swirling and in between</h2>
<p>Most wind losses here come from straight-line winds. A summer thunderstorm collapses and sends a downburst across a few square miles of Merrimack or Strafford County, flattening trees in one direction and peeling shingles off every south-facing slope. Autumn and winter storms bring hours of sustained wind rather than a single blast, which loosens fasteners, works siding off its nails and drives rain sideways under trim.</p>
<p>Tornadoes are uncommon in New Hampshire, but they happen. On July 24, 2008, an EF2 tornado tracked from Deerfield toward Ossipee, destroying homes along its path and killing one person in Deerfield. It is a reminder that rare isn’t never.</p>
<h2>Storms people here still bring up</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>October 29–30, 2017</strong> — a powerful windstorm that left a very large share of the state without power and brought down trees across the southern and central regions.</li>
<li><strong>March 2018</strong> — back-to-back nor’easters with damaging gusts, especially near the Seacoast.</li>
<li><strong>December 18, 2023</strong> — a wind and rain storm that knocked out power and caused flooding in several areas.</li>
</ul>
<p>Each of these produced a wave of claims at once, and storm-surge staffing — adjusters brought in from far away, working dozens of files a day — tends to produce rushed scopes.</p>
<h2>Wind scopes that stop too soon</h2>
<p>The most common problem we see isn’t bad pricing. It is an estimate that only covers the damage that was easy to photograph.</p>
<h3>Roofs</h3>
<p>Creased shingles that lifted and reseated, broken seal strips and nail pops don’t always show from the ground. A tab that flexed in a 60 mph gust may lie flat afterward but no longer be bonded. We test seal strength by hand and document creasing slope by slope.</p>
<h3>Siding and trim</h3>
<p>Vinyl that cracked at the lock, clapboards that split, and corner boards pulled off their fasteners all count. Replacing a single wall of discontinued siding on a Rochester ranch can leave the house two-toned, which brings matching into play.</p>
<h3>Structure</h3>
<p>Barns, sheds, carports and older garages are vulnerable to racking. A building that looks upright may have shifted off its sills. Detached structures often fall under a separate coverage limit, so we check that too.</p>
<h2>Deductibles: flat, percentage or something else</h2>
<p>Many New Hampshire homeowners policies use a flat deductible for all perils. Some, particularly closer to the coast, carry a separate wind or named-storm deductible expressed as a percentage of the dwelling limit. A 2% deductible on a $450,000 dwelling limit is $9,000 — an illustrative example, but one that shows why the type of deductible matters. Our <a href="/nh-claim-calculators/percent-deductible-converter/">percent deductible converter</a> translates percentages into dollars for your own policy.</p>
<div class="note"><b class="h">Document before the cleanup</b>Photograph everything before trees are cut up and debris is hauled away — wide shots, close-ups and the direction trees fell. Most policies require you to protect the property from further damage, so keep receipts for tarps and emergency work too.</div>
<h2>When appraisal fits a wind claim</h2>
<p>If the insurer accepts that wind caused the loss but disputes how much of the roof or siding needs replacing, that is an amount-of-loss argument, and appraisal was designed for it. If the carrier says the damage is old or caused by something else, the question gets harder; appraisal may still help, depending on the policy, but you may need legal advice. New Hampshire Insurance Appraiser can tell you which situation you’re in.</p>
<p>For trees that came down on the house, see our <a href="/nh-appraisal-services/falling-tree-claim-appraisal/">falling tree claim appraisal</a> page.</p>

<h2>What owners ask about this</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>How do I prove a microburst hit my house?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Weather service storm reports, radar archives and the pattern of damage on your property all help. Trees falling in one direction across a wide area is typical of straight-line wind, and we document it in the file.</p></div></details><details><summary>My neighbor’s roof was replaced but mine wasn’t. Why?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Different adjusters, different policies and slight differences in exposure all play a role. A side-by-side review of the two estimates, if your neighbor is willing to share, can be revealing.</p></div></details><details><summary>Does my wind deductible apply to a thunderstorm?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>It depends on the wording. Some wind or named-storm deductibles apply only to declared hurricanes or tropical storms; others apply to any windstorm. Read the endorsement carefully.</p></div></details></div>
<h2>Services that often overlap</h2><div class="related"><a href="/nh-appraisal-services/roof-and-hail-appraisal/"><small>Service</small><span>Roof Damage &amp; Hail Disputes</span><svg class="ico-sm" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M5 12h14M13 6l6 6-6 6"/></svg></a><a href="/nh-appraisal-services/falling-tree-claim-appraisal/"><small>Service</small><span>Trees Down on the Property</span><svg class="ico-sm" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M5 12h14M13 6l6 6-6 6"/></svg></a><a href="/nh-appraisal-services/tropical-storm-hurricane-appraisal-nh/"><small>Service</small><span>Tropical Remnants &amp; Coastal Storms</span><svg class="ico-sm" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M5 12h14M13 6l6 6-6 6"/></svg></a></div>
